1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:03,440
Léa: Hello everyone and welcome to The Cozy French!

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:04,800
I'm Léa.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,240
Mathieu: And I'm Mathieu.

4
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:07,500
Thank you for being with us

5
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:10,240
for a new conversation in French.

6
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:13,080
Léa: Get comfortable, at home,

7
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:15,630
on the train or during your break.

8
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:19,920
Mathieu: In fact, Léa just got back from a little train trip.

9
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:24,960
Two days, three bags, and one important question.

10
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:26,270
Léa: Which one?

11
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:29,900
Mathieu: Did your suitcase have its own ticket?

12
00:00:29,950 --> 00:00:33,240
Léa: No, but it would have liked its own room.

13
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:34,360
Mathieu: I understand.

14
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:38,520
Apparently it was planning to stay longer than you.

15
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:41,400
Léa: Today, we're talking about what we can gain

16
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:44,280
when we choose to have a little less.

17
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:48,680
Mathieu: Fewer things to carry, fewer choices to make,

18
00:00:48,680 --> 00:00:52,200
and maybe more time for what we love.

19
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:56,300
Léa: This conversation is for B1 and B2 levels.

20
00:00:56,350 --> 00:01:00,520
We'll speak slowly and explain everyday expressions.

21
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:01,240
Mathieu: By the end,

22
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:04,530
you'll be able to describe what's useful to you

23
00:01:04,530 --> 00:01:07,800
and explain why you keep an object.

24
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:11,800
Léa: You'll also be able to discuss a purchase before making up your mind.

25
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:14,200
Mathieu: No need to understand every word.

26
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:18,840
Listen to the stories, then pick a few sentences to repeat.

27
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:21,360
Léa: And don't worry, nobody is going to count

28
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:24,380
your shoes or inspect your cupboards.

29
00:01:24,430 --> 00:01:28,840
Mathieu: Good. My cupboards prefer to stay discreet.

30
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:31,420
Léa: Above all, we'll look for what helps us

31
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:33,640
enjoy our everyday life.

32
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:37,780
Mathieu: Think of an object you enjoy using.

33
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:41,240
Something simple, that really suits you.

34
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:42,260
Léa: What is it?

35
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:44,070
And what does it bring you?

36
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:46,920
Keep your answer for our final exercise.

37
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:48,580
Mathieu: You can also tell us

38
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:51,250
which country you're listening to this episode from.

39
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:55,160
Léa: I love discovering the habits behind your examples.

40
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:56,600
The same object can have

41
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:59,560
a different story in every home.

42
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:00,610
Last month,

43
00:02:00,610 --> 00:02:03,560
I went to Nantes to see a friend.

44
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:05,640
I was staying two nights at her place.

45
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:08,390
Mathieu: And you took your apartment with you?

46
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:10,120
Léa: Just a selection.

47
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:11,560
A big suitcase,

48
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:14,680
a backpack and a bag with shoes.

49
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:17,990
Mathieu: How many feet had you planned for the weekend?

50
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:20,380
Léa: Clearly more than two.

51
00:02:20,430 --> 00:02:21,260
On the tram,

52
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:25,080
I realised my suitcase was really bulky.

53
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:28,840
Mathieu: "Bulky" means it takes up a lot of space

54
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:31,590
and it's hard to move around?

55
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:35,180
Léa: Exactly. For example, this big table

56
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:37,640
is bulky in a small studio flat.

57
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:41,910
Mathieu: And in your case, the studio was moving on rails.

58
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:42,590
Léa: At the station,

59
00:02:42,590 --> 00:02:45,480
I was always looking for a spot for the bags.

60
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:48,540
Then I looked for my ticket in the wrong bag.

61
00:02:48,590 --> 00:02:53,240
Mathieu: A trip with several stages, without even changing trains.

62
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:55,880
Léa: At my friend's place, I opened the suitcase.

63
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:57,530
I had packed two jackets,

64
00:02:57,530 --> 00:03:01,160
several jumpers and a dressier outfit, just in case.

65
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:03,930
Mathieu: Just in case something happened.

66
00:03:03,930 --> 00:03:07,400
It's a way of planning for a possibility.

67
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:10,920
Léa: Yes. For example, I keep the hotel's number,

68
00:03:10,920 --> 00:03:12,440
in case I get lost.

69
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:16,060
Mathieu: But for your trip, what was the possibility?

70
00:03:16,110 --> 00:03:19,580
Léa: A surprise invitation, a change in the weather,

71
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:21,560
wanting to change outfits…

72
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:24,920
I had prepared lots of versions of the same Saturday.

73
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:26,870
Mathieu: So what did you end up doing?

74
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:28,240
Léa: We walked,

75
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:32,040
had lunch in a little restaurant and chatted at her place.

76
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:35,240
I wore the same trousers all weekend.

77
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:39,670
Mathieu: So the other clothes discovered Nantes from the bedroom.

78
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:40,100
Léa: Yes.

79
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:43,860
And on Sunday, while my friend was making coffee,

80
00:03:43,860 --> 00:03:45,820
I was putting everything back in the bags.

81
00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:48,230
Mathieu: You would have preferred to stay with her?

82
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:49,150
Léa: Of course.

83
00:03:49,150 --> 00:03:51,310
On the way home, I asked myself

84
00:03:51,310 --> 00:03:54,200
what I could do without next time.

85
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:56,600
Mathieu: To do without something

86
00:03:56,600 --> 00:04:00,120
is managing without that thing.

87
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:03,580
Léa: That's it. For example, for this trip across town,

88
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:05,480
we can do without a car.

89
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:06,950
Mathieu: So, for two days,

90
00:04:06,950 --> 00:04:10,630
maybe you could have left some clothes at home.

91
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:13,330
Léa: Yes. I needed to check the weather

92
00:04:13,330 --> 00:04:15,740
and plan suitable clothes.

93
00:04:15,790 --> 00:04:18,600
But not to take every possible option.

94
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:21,990
Mathieu: Are you going to make a little list for the next trip?

95
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:26,200
Léa: A short list, with what I actually used.

96
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:27,820
I want to spend less time

97
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:29,640
looking after my things.

98
00:04:29,640 --> 00:04:31,530
Mathieu: And more time drinking coffee

99
00:04:31,530 --> 00:04:34,040
while it's still hot.

100
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:37,160
Léa: Exactly. My friend had invited me.

101
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:39,240
Not my entire wardrobe.

102
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:42,040
Mathieu: I experienced something similar

103
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:44,220
with my board games.

104
00:04:44,270 --> 00:04:47,640
I had invited four friends on a Friday evening.

105
00:04:47,640 --> 00:04:49,190
Léa: Had you chosen a game?

106
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:50,840
Mathieu: I had brought out seven.

107
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:54,760
I wanted everyone to find something they liked.

108
00:04:54,760 --> 00:04:57,190
Léa: Seven games for a single evening?

109
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:01,000
Mathieu: Yes. A very impressive pile.

110
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,040
But to really make the most of the evening,

111
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:06,520
it wasn't very practical.

112
00:05:06,520 --> 00:05:09,280
Léa: To make the most of something

113
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:11,020
means really enjoying it

114
00:05:11,070 --> 00:05:15,000
and living that moment as fully as possible.

115
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,480
Mathieu: For example, I left my phone

116
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:21,640
in my bag to fully enjoy the concert.

117
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:24,710
Léa: And on Friday, did you manage to get started?

118
00:05:24,760 --> 00:05:28,360
Mathieu: First, I presented the seven boxes.

119
00:05:28,360 --> 00:05:30,590
Then someone asked

120
00:05:30,590 --> 00:05:34,920
whether the third game was longer than the fifth.

121
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:37,420
Léa: A dangerous question.

122
00:05:37,470 --> 00:05:41,880
Mathieu: Very dangerous. I opened two rulebooks.

123
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:44,760
One friend got up to cut some bread.

124
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:47,320
Another suggested a vote.

125
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:49,990
Léa: And how long did the vote take?

126
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:54,360
Mathieu: Long enough for the bread to be almost gone.

127
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:56,440
We had lots of choice,

128
00:05:56,440 --> 00:05:59,000
but we still weren't playing.

129
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,350
Léa: Which one did you end up picking?

130
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:04,460
Mathieu: A card game we already knew.

131
00:06:04,510 --> 00:06:08,360
We played several rounds and laughed a lot.

132
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:11,510
Léa: You could have left the big boxes on the shelf.

133
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:16,120
Mathieu: Yes. Some have been gathering dust for a long time.

134
00:06:16,120 --> 00:06:18,920
They just sit there, unused.

135
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:21,240
That's what this expression means.

136
00:06:21,240 --> 00:06:22,660
Léa: Another example:

137
00:06:22,660 --> 00:06:27,560
my rollerblades have been gathering dust since I started preferring to walk.

138
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:30,780
Mathieu: Still, I like some complicated games.

139
00:06:30,830 --> 00:06:33,240
I just want to choose a moment

140
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:36,920
when my friends feel like learning the rules.

141
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:41,400
Léa: With that group, on that evening, a familiar game was enough.

142
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:42,760
Mathieu: Exactly.

143
00:06:42,760 --> 00:06:47,800
Next time, I'll suggest two options before they arrive.

144
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:51,240
And I'll keep some room for the bread.

145
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:53,320
Léa: Room that's easier to find

146
00:06:53,320 --> 00:06:56,700
when the table doesn't look like a shop.

147
00:06:56,750 --> 00:06:57,730
What strikes me

148
00:06:57,730 --> 00:07:00,840
is that we both wanted to be ready.

149
00:07:00,840 --> 00:07:04,840
Me for every situation, you for every craving.

150
00:07:04,840 --> 00:07:05,470
Mathieu: Yes.

151
00:07:05,470 --> 00:07:09,960
And I find it hard to give up a possibility,

152
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:14,360
even when it doesn't really interest me that day.

153
00:07:14,360 --> 00:07:16,760
Léa: To find it hard to do something

154
00:07:16,760 --> 00:07:19,560
means finding that action difficult.

155
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:23,240
Mathieu: For example, I find it hard to choose a film

156
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:26,220
when everyone is waiting for my answer.

157
00:07:26,270 --> 00:07:28,840
Léa: Me, I'm sometimes afraid of regretting things.

158
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:33,560
So I keep, I add, I plan another solution.

159
00:07:33,560 --> 00:07:36,120
Mathieu: It's useful in some situations.

160
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:39,800
A jacket for the rain can really come in handy.

161
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:42,420
Léa: Yes. The question is to look at

162
00:07:42,420 --> 00:07:45,080
what suits our real life.

163
00:07:45,080 --> 00:07:47,800
Mathieu: And also to keep what we love.

164
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:50,600
I'm attached to my old chess set,

165
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:53,500
even if I don't play every week.

166
00:07:53,550 --> 00:07:54,920
Léa: To be attached to something

167
00:07:54,920 --> 00:07:59,000
means giving it importance, being attached to it.

168
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,140
Mathieu: For example, my flatmate is attached to this poster

169
00:08:03,140 --> 00:08:06,680
because it reminds her of her first concert.

170
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:09,880
Léa: So we can keep an object for its usefulness,

171
00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:13,880
but also for the pleasure or the memories it brings.

172
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:15,970
Mathieu: And being satisfied with less,

173
00:08:15,970 --> 00:08:19,340
doesn't that risk becoming sad?

174
00:08:19,390 --> 00:08:21,530
Léa: To be satisfied with something

175
00:08:21,530 --> 00:08:23,880
means considering that it's enough.

176
00:08:23,880 --> 00:08:27,560
But everyone should be able to choose what's enough for them.

177
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:30,140
Mathieu: For example, for our walk,

178
00:08:30,140 --> 00:08:31,570
we made do

179
00:08:31,570 --> 00:08:35,640
with a small bag with some water and a snack.

180
00:08:35,640 --> 00:08:38,600
Léa: Needs vary from person to person.

181
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:40,520
Some already have too little

182
00:08:40,520 --> 00:08:43,720
and above all need more resources.

183
00:08:43,720 --> 00:08:44,110
Mathieu: Yes.

184
00:08:44,110 --> 00:08:45,880
For me, today,

185
00:08:45,880 --> 00:08:48,860
it's about choosing among my options.

186
00:08:48,910 --> 00:08:51,400
Not deciding for other people.

187
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:53,560
Léa: Let's try a little scene.

188
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:55,420
We're preparing a picnic

189
00:08:55,420 --> 00:08:57,880
and you're looking at an online shop.

190
00:08:57,880 --> 00:09:00,280
Mathieu: You can answer before me.

191
00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:01,650
Léa: I found a basket

192
00:09:01,650 --> 00:09:04,680
with matching plates and glasses.

193
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:06,710
Shall we order it for Sunday?

194
00:09:06,760 --> 00:09:07,590
Mathieu: Your turn!

195
00:09:07,640 --> 00:09:10,680
You already have something to carry the meal in.

196
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:16,710
How would you suggest checking before buying?

197
00:09:16,760 --> 00:09:18,780
Take a few seconds.

198
00:09:18,830 --> 00:09:23,030
Léa: You can start with: "Do we already have…?"

199
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:26,600
Mathieu: Do we already have something that could do the job?

200
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:29,640
I have a sturdy bag and a few boxes.

201
00:09:29,640 --> 00:09:30,560
Léa: That's true.

202
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:33,800
And I have cutlery and a big tablecloth.

203
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:37,960
The basket might be an impulse purchase.

204
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:42,100
Mathieu: An impulse purchase is a purchase decided on the spot,

205
00:09:42,100 --> 00:09:44,140
without really thinking first.

206
00:09:44,190 --> 00:09:47,660
Léa: For example, these glasses were an impulse purchase

207
00:09:47,660 --> 00:09:49,800
during a walk in town.

208
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:51,430
Mathieu: Do you use them?

209
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:54,390
Léa: Yes, but not all my quick decisions

210
00:09:54,390 --> 00:09:56,680
turn out so well.

211
00:09:56,680 --> 00:10:00,630
Back to Sunday: will your bag be big enough?

212
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:02,520
Mathieu: Yes, for the food.

213
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:04,120
And for the drinks,

214
00:10:04,120 --> 00:10:07,800
I can borrow a cool bag from my neighbour.

215
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:08,840
Léa: OK.

216
00:10:08,840 --> 00:10:13,420
Before ordering, we can weigh the pros and cons.

217
00:10:13,470 --> 00:10:15,400
Mathieu: That's examining the advantages

218
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:19,240
and disadvantages to make a decision.

219
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:22,280
Léa: For example, before moving flats,

220
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:26,040
we weighed the pros and cons.

221
00:10:26,040 --> 00:10:28,440
Mathieu: As for the basket, it's pretty.

222
00:10:28,440 --> 00:10:33,720
But you have to pay for it, store it and carry it.

223
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:37,320
Léa: So, for this time, let's use what we have.

224
00:10:37,320 --> 00:10:41,340
We can think about buying something if the need comes back.

225
00:10:41,390 --> 00:10:42,320
Mathieu: Very good.

226
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:46,600
And now, a truly important decision:

227
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:48,950
who's bringing the cheese?

228
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,200
Léa: I think you just volunteered.

229
00:10:52,200 --> 00:10:56,390
Mathieu: If I want to declutter at home, where do I start?

230
00:10:56,440 --> 00:11:01,160
I look at my living room and I already see several projects.

231
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:02,420
Léa: To declutter

232
00:11:02,420 --> 00:11:06,440
is to remove what takes up space for no reason

233
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:10,860
to make a space more pleasant or more practical.

234
00:11:10,910 --> 00:11:14,380
Mathieu: For example, she decluttered her hallway

235
00:11:14,380 --> 00:11:17,640
so she could open the door easily.

236
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:20,400
Léa: You can start with one specific spot.

237
00:11:20,400 --> 00:11:24,280
The little corner where you put your mail, for example.

238
00:11:24,280 --> 00:11:27,800
Mathieu: Do I sort my flatmate's mail too?

239
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:32,280
Léa: No, you leave his things so he can decide himself.

240
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:35,580
You can start with your own papers.

241
00:11:35,630 --> 00:11:37,180
Mathieu: So, to sort through:

242
00:11:37,180 --> 00:11:40,420
looking at things and separating them

243
00:11:40,420 --> 00:11:42,680
according to what you'll do with them.

244
00:11:42,680 --> 00:11:45,430
Léa: Yes. For example, we sorted through the photos

245
00:11:45,430 --> 00:11:49,080
before making an album.

246
00:11:49,080 --> 00:11:51,520
Mathieu: For my mail, I can separate

247
00:11:51,520 --> 00:11:53,550
the documents to keep,

248
00:11:53,550 --> 00:11:58,700
the ones to deal with and the ads to recycle.

249
00:11:58,750 --> 00:12:00,450
Léa: That's it. For an object,

250
00:12:00,450 --> 00:12:03,490
you can ask yourself when you last used it

251
00:12:03,490 --> 00:12:06,280
and whether you really want to keep it.

252
00:12:06,280 --> 00:12:08,700
Mathieu: I could also find someone

253
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:11,720
who'd like a game I no longer play.

254
00:12:11,720 --> 00:12:14,920
Léa: Yes, if it's complete and in good condition.

255
00:12:14,920 --> 00:12:17,000
But you can take the time

256
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,560
to choose a suitable solution.

257
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:22,680
Mathieu: And to avoid adding what's unnecessary?

258
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:25,000
It's what goes beyond our needs,

259
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,460
what isn't necessary in a situation.

260
00:12:28,510 --> 00:12:29,650
Léa: Exactly.

261
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:32,600
For example, for this short presentation,

262
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:35,320
the animations were unnecessary.

263
00:12:35,320 --> 00:12:37,720
The pictures were enough.

264
00:12:37,720 --> 00:12:41,510
Mathieu: I can wait before ordering a new object.

265
00:12:41,510 --> 00:12:45,480
Then check whether I already have one that works.

266
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:47,720
Léa: And if you really need it,

267
00:12:47,720 --> 00:12:49,790
you can look for what fits

268
00:12:49,790 --> 00:12:52,200
your use and your budget.

269
00:12:52,200 --> 00:12:53,890
Mathieu: For my game nights,

270
00:12:53,890 --> 00:12:57,910
I mainly want to make room for conversation.

271
00:12:57,960 --> 00:13:01,400
Léa: To make room means freeing up a space.

272
00:13:01,400 --> 00:13:04,190
You can also use this expression

273
00:13:04,190 --> 00:13:06,520
for time or activities.

274
00:13:06,520 --> 00:13:09,340
Mathieu: For example, I moved a meeting

275
00:13:09,340 --> 00:13:13,240
to make room for a personal appointment.

276
00:13:13,240 --> 00:13:16,440
Léa: Me, I'm going to pack a simpler bag.

277
00:13:16,440 --> 00:13:18,050
It'll be a way of keeping

278
00:13:18,050 --> 00:13:19,800
my attention for my friend.

279
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:23,340
Mathieu: And I'll start with a single shelf.

280
00:13:23,390 --> 00:13:24,950
I know my enthusiasm:

281
00:13:24,950 --> 00:13:28,680
it could order three pieces of furniture to store fewer things.

282
00:13:28,680 --> 00:13:31,400
Léa: Let's go back to your object from the beginning.

283
00:13:31,400 --> 00:13:35,910
You can say: "I keep… because…

284
00:13:35,960 --> 00:13:37,960
I use it for…"

285
00:13:37,960 --> 00:13:41,800
Mathieu: I keep my chess set because I love its board.

286
00:13:41,800 --> 00:13:43,730
I use it to play with a friend

287
00:13:43,730 --> 00:13:45,480
when we have time.

288
00:13:45,480 --> 00:13:47,170
Léa: I keep my little backpack

289
00:13:47,170 --> 00:13:49,340
because it's comfortable.

290
00:13:49,390 --> 00:13:53,320
I use it for walks and short trips.

291
00:13:53,320 --> 00:13:54,360
Mathieu: Your turn.

292
00:13:54,360 --> 00:13:57,240
Say your two sentences out loud.

293
00:13:57,240 --> 00:14:00,200
It can be a very ordinary object.

294
00:14:00,200 --> 00:14:02,680
Léa: Now, try:

295
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:07,720
"I could have less… to have more…"

296
00:14:07,720 --> 00:14:10,840
Mathieu: I could have fewer games on the table

297
00:14:10,840 --> 00:14:13,500
to have more time together.

298
00:14:13,550 --> 00:14:17,620
Léa: I could have fewer clothes in my suitcase

299
00:14:17,620 --> 00:14:21,320
to have more freedom when I travel.

300
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:23,800
Mathieu: Your answer can also be about choices,

301
00:14:23,800 --> 00:14:27,160
notifications or activities.

302
00:14:27,160 --> 00:14:28,840
Take a concrete example.

303
00:14:28,840 --> 00:14:31,280
Léa: Even a single sentence will help you

304
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:34,280
talk about your priorities in French.

305
00:14:34,280 --> 00:14:37,320
Mathieu: Let's go back over five expressions from the conversation.

306
00:14:37,320 --> 00:14:39,800
Listen, then repeat with us.

307
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:42,540
Léa: First, "au cas où" – just in case.

308
00:14:42,590 --> 00:14:43,530
In my suitcase,

309
00:14:43,530 --> 00:14:47,960
I had planned clothes for several possible situations.

310
00:14:47,960 --> 00:14:51,240
Mathieu: You prepare something for a possibility.

311
00:14:51,240 --> 00:14:55,040
New example: I take a copy of the document,

312
00:14:55,090 --> 00:14:58,520
in case the original is hard to read.

313
00:14:58,520 --> 00:15:01,990
Léa: Let's repeat: au cas où.

314
00:15:02,040 --> 00:15:04,620
When you add a clause with a verb,

315
00:15:04,620 --> 00:15:07,800
you normally use the conditional.

316
00:15:07,800 --> 00:15:10,300
Mathieu: Next, "se passer de".

317
00:15:10,350 --> 00:15:13,160
You could travel without some of your things.

318
00:15:13,160 --> 00:15:15,880
Léa: It means doing without something.

319
00:15:15,880 --> 00:15:18,340
Example: for this recipe,

320
00:15:18,340 --> 00:15:22,840
I can do without a blender and use a fork.

321
00:15:22,840 --> 00:15:25,590
Mathieu: Let's repeat: se passer de.

322
00:15:25,640 --> 00:15:29,640
You can start with "I can do without…"

323
00:15:29,640 --> 00:15:33,510
Léa: Third expression: "prendre la poussière".

324
00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:36,550
Some game boxes stayed for a long time

325
00:15:36,550 --> 00:15:39,100
on your shelf without being opened.

326
00:15:39,150 --> 00:15:41,320
Mathieu: They were no longer being used.

327
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:44,620
Example: this dictionary has been gathering dust

328
00:15:44,620 --> 00:15:47,640
since I started using the digital version.

329
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:50,950
Léa: Let's repeat: prendre la poussière.

330
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:56,040
The expression describes an object that stays forgotten or unused.

331
00:15:56,040 --> 00:15:59,910
Mathieu: Fourth expression: "tenir à quelque chose".

332
00:15:59,960 --> 00:16:02,280
I'm attached to my chess set

333
00:16:02,280 --> 00:16:05,880
and my flatmate is attached to her poster.

334
00:16:05,880 --> 00:16:08,300
Léa: It means being attached to it.

335
00:16:08,350 --> 00:16:10,870
Example: I'm attached to this notebook,

336
00:16:10,870 --> 00:16:14,680
because it holds the ideas for my first show.

337
00:16:14,680 --> 00:16:18,070
Mathieu: Let's repeat: tenir à quelque chose.

338
00:16:18,120 --> 00:16:21,350
You can explain the reason with "parce que".

339
00:16:21,400 --> 00:16:24,950
Léa: Finally, "peser le pour et le contre".

340
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:26,680
Before buying the basket,

341
00:16:26,680 --> 00:16:30,520
we looked at the advantages and disadvantages.

342
00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:34,460
Mathieu: Example: before accepting this training course,

343
00:16:34,460 --> 00:16:36,770
I'm going to weigh the pros and cons

344
00:16:36,770 --> 00:16:38,680
with my manager.

345
00:16:38,680 --> 00:16:42,710
Léa: Let's repeat: peser le pour et le contre.

346
00:16:42,760 --> 00:16:45,060
It can be useful for a purchase,

347
00:16:45,060 --> 00:16:48,040
a project or a big change.

348
00:16:48,040 --> 00:16:53,800
Mathieu: Now choose an expression and imagine your own situation.

349
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:57,420
You can write it down, then say it out loud.

350
00:16:57,470 --> 00:17:00,900
Léa: An example linked to your life will be more personal

351
00:17:00,900 --> 00:17:04,280
than a sentence you repeat without thinking.

352
00:17:04,280 --> 00:17:06,020
For your comment,

353
00:17:06,020 --> 00:17:10,630
complete this sentence: "I keep… because…"

354
00:17:10,680 --> 00:17:13,880
We'd love to discover your favourite objects.

355
00:17:13,880 --> 00:17:17,640
Mathieu: You can also write what you'd like to simplify,

356
00:17:17,640 --> 00:17:20,940
and what it would let you enjoy more.

357
00:17:20,990 --> 00:17:23,320
Léa: If you enjoyed this conversation,

358
00:17:23,320 --> 00:17:25,400
subscribe to The Cozy French

359
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:28,120
to keep learning with us.

360
00:17:28,120 --> 00:17:32,280
Mathieu: And to practise, go back over a few lines from the picnic.

361
00:17:32,280 --> 00:17:34,760
You can play both characters.

362
00:17:34,760 --> 00:17:37,000
Léa: Thank you for sharing this moment.

363
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,910
Take care and see you very soon!

364
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:43,230
Mathieu: One last question: for your next trip,

365
00:17:43,230 --> 00:17:45,430
is your big suitcase coming with you?

366
00:17:45,480 --> 00:17:48,600
Léa: No, this time it's staying at home.

367
00:17:48,600 --> 00:17:50,780
It's taking a few days off.

368
00:17:50,830 --> 00:17:51,510
Mathieu: Very good.

369
00:17:51,510 --> 00:17:55,920
Its train ticket was surely more expensive than yours.
